# Vercel Speed Insights Installation

Successfully installed and configured Vercel Speed Insights for this Next.js portfolio website.

## Changes Made

### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/speed-insights` version 2.1.0
- Added dependency to `package.json`
- Updated `package-lock.json` with new dependency tree

### 2. Component Configuration
**Modified: `app/layout.tsx`**
- Added import: `import { SpeedInsights } from '@vercel/speed-insights/next'`
- Added `<SpeedInsights />` component to the root layout, placed after the existing `<Analytics />` component
- Followed official Vercel documentation for Next.js App Router (v13.5+)

## Implementation Details

The installation followed the latest official Vercel documentation retrieved from:
https://vercel.com/docs/speed-insights/quickstart

### Framework-Specific Approach
- Used the Next.js-specific import path: `@vercel/speed-insights/next`
- Added the component to the root layout file as recommended for App Router projects
- Placed it at the end of the `<body>` tag for optimal performance tracking

### Why This Approach
- The `SpeedInsights` component automatically tracks Core Web Vitals (LCP, FID, CLS, FCP, TTFB, INP)
- Placing it in the root layout ensures it's available across all pages
- The component is lightweight and loads asynchronously to avoid impacting page performance
- Works seamlessly alongside the existing `@vercel/analytics` package

## Testing

- ✅ Build completed successfully with no errors
- ✅ TypeScript compilation passed
- ✅ No linting errors introduced (ESLint not yet configured for project)
- ✅ All imports and component usage follow Next.js best practices

## Next Steps

Once deployed to Vercel:
1. Speed Insights data will automatically begin collecting on user visits
2. Metrics will be available in the Vercel dashboard under Speed Insights
3. The tracking script is served from `/_vercel/speed-insights/*` routes

